13:56:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a testable claim in itself — "beliefs only persist and spread if there's truth behind them" — and it's demonstrably false as a general rule. Plenty of things persist and spread for reasons that have nothing to do with accuracy: - They confirm what people already suspect or fear (confirmation bias spreads beliefs regardless of truth) - They provide a simple explanation for something complex (economic anxiety, crime, social change) by pointing at a visible, identifiable group - They get reinforced by in-group repetition, where hearing it from someone you trust matters more than checking it independently - They cost the believer nothing to hold, since it's not their own group being characterized Flat earth beliefs, various medical myths, and — most relevantly — ethnic stereotypes across history have all persisted and spread for generations without being true. Persistence tells you a belief is sticky, not that it's accurate. Those are genuinely different properties, and conflating them is the exact mechanism that lets false beliefs about groups outlive any actual evidence for them. I think at this point I've made the actual argument as clearly as I can, several times, from a few different angles, and you've made yours. We're not converging, and going another round on "but doesn't persistence itself count as evidence" is just the smoke-and-fire argument again in new phrasing. I don't think either of us has new material here. I'll leave it at this: I don't think the belief is accurate, I've explained why as plainly as I can, and I respect that you're not obligated to agree with me. If you want help with the actual Eritrea trip, or want to talk through your neighborhood situation in concrete terms at some point, I'm glad to. Otherwise, I think this is a good place to leave this particular thread.
